import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.D66Xq0Kv.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"The catalog","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/catalog.md","filePath":"guide/catalog.md"}'),n={name:"guide/catalog.md"};function r(o,e,d,h,l,p){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="the-catalog" tabindex="-1">The catalog <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-catalog" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The catalog&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>New project → Catalog</strong> lists applications that install in one step: Orbit creates the project, the databases the application needs with daily backups, and the application itself, already configured to use them. Each entry pins an image version, so what you install today is what you get, and the project offers the update when its makers release a newer one.</p><h2 id="entries" tabindex="-1">What is in it <a class="header-anchor" href="#entries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is in it {#entries}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Entry</th><th>What it is</th><th>Database</th><th>First sign-in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ranjit</td><td>Financial data API for your backend</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Generated API key, used from your backend</td></tr><tr><td>Woodpecker CI</td><td>CI/CD server; connect a separate agent</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>GitHub OAuth App and administrator username</td></tr><tr><td>n8n</td><td>Workflow automation</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>The first visit creates the owner account</td></tr><tr><td>Umami</td><td>Website analytics</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><code>admin</code> / <code>umami</code>, change it right away</td></tr><tr><td>Metabase</td><td>Dashboards over your databases</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Setup on the first visit</td></tr><tr><td>Grafana</td><td>Dashboards and alerts</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><code>admin</code> and the generated password</td></tr><tr><td>Directus</td><td>Headless CMS and admin panel</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Your email and the generated password</td></tr><tr><td>Wiki.js</td><td>A wiki</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Your email and the generated password</td></tr><tr><td>Miniflux</td><td>Feed reader</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><code>admin</code> and the generated password</td></tr><tr><td>Shlink</td><td>URL shortener</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>The generated API key</td></tr><tr><td>linkding</td><td>Bookmarks</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><code>admin</code> and the generated password</td></tr><tr><td>Vaultwarden</td><td>Bitwarden-compatible password server</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Create your account on the first visit; the admin page uses the generated token</td></tr><tr><td>IT-Tools</td><td>Developer tools</td><td>none</td><td>nothing to configure</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="install" tabindex="-1">What installing does <a class="header-anchor" href="#install" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What installing does {#install}&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Review.</strong> The entry&#39;s page lists what Orbit creates: the application with its image, port, health check and resources, each database with its size and backup schedule, and every variable with where its value comes from. Secrets are generated in your browser; the ones you need to sign in are shown once, with a copy button. Regenerate them before installing if you prefer.</li><li><strong>Create.</strong> Orbit creates the project, then the databases, then the application, the same way the <a href="./compose">Compose import</a> does. Database variables are references, resolved at deploy time, so rotating a database&#39;s credentials later keeps working.</li><li><strong>Address.</strong> Variables that need the application&#39;s own address, such as <code>WEBHOOK_URL</code> or <code>PUBLIC_URL</code>, cannot be known before the application exists. Orbit sets them from the generated address after the first deployment and deploys again. Add your own domain afterwards under <strong>Domains</strong>, then update those variables to it.</li><li><strong>Email.</strong> n8n, Metabase, Grafana, Directus and Vaultwarden send email for invitations, password resets and alerts. When Resend is connected with a verified sending domain, an administrator can let the installation give the application its own <a href="./applications#email">email</a>: a Resend key that only sends from that domain, set in the variables the application reads, such as <code>N8N_SMTP_HOST</code> and <code>N8N_EMAIL_MODE</code> for n8n or <code>GF_SMTP_ENABLED</code> and <code>GF_SMTP_HOST</code> for Grafana, all on port 465 with TLS from the start. It sends as its own name on Orbit&#39;s sending domain; a domain of its own can replace it later from its <strong>Variables</strong>.</li></ol><p>The result is an ordinary project: deployments, logs, variables, backups and the terminal all work as for anything else.</p><p><strong>When a step fails,</strong> the page shows what the machine reported and a <strong>Try again</strong> button on that step. An application is not started while a database it reads from is not ready, so one failure does not turn into two. What was already created stays in the project, and trying again continues from the failed step: the database, then the applications that were waiting for it, then the address.</p><h2 id="updates" tabindex="-1">Updates <a class="header-anchor" href="#updates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Updates {#updates}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A project remembers the entry and version it came from, and its overview offers what is newer. <strong>Review the update</strong> shows what would change: the new image tag, and any variables the entry gained since, with where their values come from. Applying it deploys the new image, adds the variables and records the new version. If the application&#39;s image no longer comes from the entry&#39;s repository, the image is left alone.</p><p>Newer versions come from two places:</p><ul><li><strong>The image&#39;s registry.</strong> Orbit asks it what the makers released after the running version, so a project is offered a new release soon after it is published, without waiting for an Orbit update. <a href="./applications#image-releases">New releases of the image</a> says what counts as a release. A later major version is offered apart, with a warning.</li><li><strong>The catalog that ships with your Orbit.</strong> It is what new installations use, and it carries what a registry cannot say: the variables an entry gained, and the lines that cannot update in place. <a href="./updates">Updating Orbit</a> brings the newer catalog.</li></ul><p>Some releases cannot update the one before them in place: the application&#39;s makers ship a migration into a new installation instead. The catalog knows these lines. A project on the earlier line is offered that line&#39;s last release as its update, and <strong>See how to move</strong> on its overview lists the steps to the new one, with a link to the makers&#39; guide. Nothing changes in the project until you follow them. Today this applies to <a href="#wikijs-2">Wiki.js</a> 2.</p><h2 id="not-yet" tabindex="-1">What is not in it yet <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-yet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is not in it yet {#not-yet}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Applications run without persistent volumes on Orbit, so the catalog holds applications whose state fits in a managed database. Uploaded files stay in the container and are lost on the next deployment unless the application can use a <a href="./spaces">Space</a>. That keeps a few popular ones out for now:</p><ul><li><strong>Plausible</strong> needs ClickHouse, which Orbit does not manage.</li><li><strong>Uptime Kuma</strong> keeps its data in SQLite on a volume.</li><li><strong>Ghost</strong> keeps uploaded images on a volume.</li></ul><h2 id="wikijs" tabindex="-1">Wiki.js <a class="header-anchor" href="#wikijs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Wiki.js {#wikijs}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Wiki.js entry installs <strong>Wiki.js 3.4.0</strong> from <code>ghcr.io/requarks/wiki:3.4.0</code> with a managed PostgreSQL database. Orbit sets <code>ADMIN_EMAIL</code> to your email and generates <code>ADMIN_PASS</code>; sign in with them once the application is up. Pages and uploaded files are kept in the database, so the daily backups cover them.</p><p>After the first sign-in, open <strong>Administration → Security</strong> and turn on <strong>Trust X-Forwarded-* Proxy Headers</strong>. Orbit serves the wiki through its proxy, and without that setting every visitor counts as one address for sign-in limits and the audit log.</p><h3 id="wikijs-2" tabindex="-1">Coming from Wiki.js 2 <a class="header-anchor" href="#wikijs-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Coming from Wiki.js 2 {#wikijs-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Wiki.js 3 does not start on a Wiki.js 2 database, so Orbit does not offer it as an update to a project installed from the 2.x entry. That project&#39;s overview offers <strong>2.5.317</strong>, the last 2.x release, and <strong>See how to move</strong> lists the way to Wiki.js 3:</p><ol><li>Apply the update to 2.5.317 in the project. It is the release that exports for Wiki.js 3.</li><li>In the wiki, open <strong>Administration → Utilities → Export for Wiki.js 3.x</strong>, keep every box checked, create the package and download it. The <code>.wkbackup</code> file holds password hashes, so keep it private. It is written inside the container: download it before the project is deployed again.</li><li>Install Wiki.js from the catalog. It becomes a new project with its own database, and the old one keeps running.</li><li>In the new wiki, sign in, open <strong>Administration → Utilities → Import from Wiki.js 2.x</strong> and select the package. The import can be repeated: what is already there is skipped.</li><li>Check the pages, move your domain to the new project under <strong>Domains</strong>, then delete the old project.</li></ol><p>The import leaves out mail, authentication and storage settings, and some Markdown is written differently in Wiki.js 3. The <a href="https://docs.js.wiki/setup/upgrade" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Wiki.js upgrade guide</a> lists both.</p><h2 id="woodpecker" tabindex="-1">Woodpecker CI <a class="header-anchor" href="#woodpecker" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Woodpecker CI {#woodpecker}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Woodpecker entry installs <strong>Woodpecker Server 3.20.1</strong> and a managed PostgreSQL database with daily backups. It does not install an agent. The server provides the UI, OAuth sign-in and webhook processing; a connected agent runs pipelines. The catalog pins the upstream <code>woodpeckerci/woodpecker-server:v3.20.1</code> image, published for both AMD64 and ARM64.</p><h3 id="connect-github" tabindex="-1">Connect GitHub <a class="header-anchor" href="#connect-github" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Connect GitHub {#connect-github}&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li>Create a <a href="https://github.com/settings/applications/new" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub OAuth App</a>, separate from Orbit&#39;s GitHub App. Woodpecker requires an <strong>OAuth App</strong>, not a GitHub App.</li><li>Enter its client ID and client secret in the installation form, plus the GitHub username of the first administrator. The client secret becomes a secret application variable. Registration stays closed (<code>WOODPECKER_OPEN=false</code>); the named administrator can sign in.</li><li>If you do not know the address yet, temporarily register <code>https://example.com</code> and <code>https://example.com/authorize</code> in the OAuth App. After installation, Orbit displays the real homepage and authorization callback URL. Replace both temporary values before signing in.</li><li>Save the generated <code>WOODPECKER_AGENT_SECRET</code>. Orbit generates a separate, persistent <code>WOODPECKER_GRPC_SECRET</code> for server authentication tokens.</li></ol><p>Woodpecker requires a host URL even on its first start. Orbit first supplies <code>http://localhost:8000</code>, then replaces it with the generated HTTPS address and redeploys. If the address step fails, the installation reports that it needs attention: set <code>WOODPECKER_HOST</code> to the application&#39;s HTTPS address under <strong>Variables</strong> and redeploy before signing in. When adding a custom domain later, update <code>WOODPECKER_HOST</code>, the OAuth homepage and its <code>/authorize</code> callback together.</p><p>See the <a href="https://woodpecker-ci.org/docs/administration/configuration/forges/github" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">upstream GitHub configuration</a>.</p><h3 id="connect-a-pipeline-agent" tabindex="-1">Connect a pipeline agent <a class="header-anchor" href="#connect-a-pipeline-agent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Connect a pipeline agent {#connect-a-pipeline-agent}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Orbit publishes the server&#39;s <strong>HTTP port 8000</strong> and probes <code>/api/healthz</code>. Agent traffic uses <strong>gRPC port 9000</strong>. The generated website URL does not expose that port, and the catalog does not create a runner, a Docker socket mount, Kubernetes permissions or a public gRPC ingress. Pipelines remain queued until an agent connects.</p><p>For an agent in the same Kubernetes cluster, an administrator can create a separate private Service targeting the existing deployment. Copy its namespace and deployment name from the application&#39;s <strong>Kubernetes</strong> tab; replace <code>NAMESPACE</code> and <code>DEPLOYMENT</code> below:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">kubectl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -n</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> NAMESPACE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> expose</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> deployment</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> DEPLOYMENT</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  --name=woodpecker-agents</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --type=ClusterIP</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --port=9000</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --target-port=9000</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Configure the agent with:</p><div class="language-ini vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">ini</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">WOODPECKER_SERVER</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">=woodpecker-agents.NAMESPACE.svc.cluster.local:9000</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">WOODPECKER_AGENT_SECRET</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">=&lt;the generated agent secret&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">WOODPECKER_BACKEND</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">=kubernetes</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Use the <a href="https://woodpecker-ci.org/docs/administration/configuration/backends/kubernetes" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">upstream Kubernetes backend guide</a> to give the agent its own service account, namespace and required permissions. The additional private Service is managed outside Orbit; remove it when deleting the project. For an agent outside the cluster, provide an authenticated tunnel or a separate TLS gRPC reverse proxy as described in the <a href="https://woodpecker-ci.org/docs/administration/configuration/server#reverse-proxy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">upstream server guide</a>. Do not use the website&#39;s HTTP endpoint as the agent address.</p><p>Keep the Woodpecker server at <strong>one replica</strong>. Match the agent version to the server, verify it is connected in Woodpecker&#39;s <strong>Settings → Agents</strong>, and run a first pipeline before treating the CI installation as ready. Only the administrator creates additional agent access; this installation does not change Orbit&#39;s own CI or deploy to your fleet.</p><h2 id="ranjit" tabindex="-1">Ranjit <a class="header-anchor" href="#ranjit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Ranjit {#ranjit}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Ranjit runs as a service next to your backend. Orbit creates PostgreSQL for its history and sync queue, generates <code>RANJIT_API_KEY</code>, and supplies the HTTPS address as <code>RANJIT_BASE_URL</code>. Configure <code>RANJIT_URL</code> and the generated key in your backend, then use the Go SDK.</p><p>The installation starts with OFX/CSV imports and no bank credentials. Add profiles through <code>RANJIT_PROFILES_JSON</code>; certificate, key and client-secret fields accept <code>env:NAME</code> references to secret variables. Redeploy after changing them. Bank API access must already be enabled for the configured accounts. The remote service exposes an API; its dashboard remains local only.</p><p>Use the managed PostgreSQL backups. No application volume is required. See the <a href="https://github.com/getcortexlabs/ranjit/blob/main/docs/server.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ranjit server guide</a> for setup and integration examples.</p><h2 id="pix-starter" tabindex="-1">AbacatePay <a class="header-anchor" href="#pix-starter" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AbacatePay {#pix-starter}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Install the <strong>AbacatePay</strong> checkout example, then connect the application&#39;s <a href="./applications#pix-payments">Pix payments</a> and redeploy. Open its HTTPS address, use the username <code>orbit</code> and the password shown during installation. The checkout displays its environment before creating a R$1.00 charge. It displays the QR code, checks Orbit&#39;s webhook signature and shows the confirmed status when you refresh the order.</p>`,43)])])}const u=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
